  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "An English stage form launched by John Gay's The Beggar's Opera (1728), in which spoken dialogue alternates with short songs set to well-known tunes, broadside airs and borrowed opera melodies, with an overture and basses arranged by Johann Christoph Pepusch. Its satire of Italian opera started a vogue for ballad operas in London and the provinces over the following decade, and modern productions and recordings use either period instruments or later arrangements.",
